 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Parts of Jakarta are sinking up to twenty five centimeters a year.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's faster than the sea is rising, many times faster.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And it's so serious that Indonesia is building a brand new capital on another island.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So what's pulling a whole city down? Water. Or rather, missing water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Jakarta sits on soft layers of clay and sand, full of water, like a wet sponge.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Many homes and factories there don't get piped water.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So they drill their own wells, and pump water up from underground.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Pull the water out of a sponge, and it shrinks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The clay squeezes together, and everything on top goes down with it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "You can't pump it back up. Squeezed clay stays squeezed.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Near the coast, some streets now sit below the sea. Walls hold the ocean back.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Some neighborhoods have dropped more than two meters.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The real fix is boring. Pipe clean water to every house, and close the wells.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Tokyo did exactly that decades ago, and it mostly stopped sinking.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
